From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: glacier <hyouga.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 12:57:27 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Bucket the entity slice index on Z as well as X

With X alone a query still widened its range by the widest entity the slice had held,
so on a crowded slice it read about a quarter of it and threw most of that away on the
Y and Z tests. collideWithNearbyEntities reaches this for every living entity every
tick, and a profile of the saturating bench put the scan itself at 12.4% of the server
thread - the largest single leaf frame in the server.

The Z rows are four blocks deep while the X columns stay one block wide. That is not
arbitrary: a one-block Z row was tried first and measured -0.13%, 3/6 wins, a flat null.
The narrowing was real, but an entity that moves diagonally then crosses a bucket almost
every tick, and Entity.move updates the index several times per tick through
setEntityBoundingBox. Four-block rows keep most of the narrowing while crossing a
quarter as often, and that is the whole difference between the two shapes.

The index stays exact: a bucket is (x << 2) | z, both clamped into the chunk the way the
X column already was, and clamping is monotone so the widened range still covers every
entity whose minimum corner could reach the box. Verified with debugVerifyAthenaIndexes
against the flat slice walk - 6,909,814 bucketed entity checks, no mismatch.

Measured +1.83% TPS, 6/6 pairwise wins, at 30,000 golems with four bots.

